course: dictionary
lesson: 15529
title: Successive: one after another
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Successive means coming one after another, in a row, with no break in between. «The team won three successive games» means the first game was a win, the second was a win, and the third was a win, with nothing in between.
explain: The word is often used with days, years and numbers. «It rained on five successive days» means it rained on day one, day two, day three, day four and day five, with no dry day in between. In maths, successive numbers such as 4, 5 and 6 follow each other.
explain: Use the swap test to check. Put «in a row» in place of successive. «Three wins in a row» still makes sense, so successive means one after another.
fact: Successive means coming or happening one after another, without a break.
fact: «Successive» is an adjective, so it describes a noun such as days, wins or years.
fact: «Successive numbers» such as 7, 8 and 9 are also called consecutive numbers.
fact: «For the third successive time» means three times in a row.
fact: Do not mix up successive with successful: successful means having achieved what you wanted.
exercise: «The team won five successive matches.» What does successive mean here? | *One after another, with no break | Played in another town | Very fast and sudden | Won by a large score
exercise: Which set of numbers is successive, following one after another? | 9, 4, 1 | 2, 4, 8 | 4, 6, 9 | *4, 5, 6
exercise: «It rained on three successive days.» Which phrase means the same? | On three days, with a dry day between each | On three days that were sunny | On three days in summer | *On three days in a row
test: «The bus was late for the fourth successive morning.» What does successive mean? | Early for once | Late for the first time | *One after another, in a row | At different times of the day
test: Which word could replace successive in «successive years»? | Unusual | Random | *Consecutive | Separate
exam: Ella says successive and successful mean the same thing. Is she right? | Yes, both words mean winning | No, successive means very old | Yes, both words mean very quiet | *No, successive means one after another, and successful means having achieved what you wanted
